  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The secondary structure of protein refers to the shape in which a long polypeptide chain can exist. They are found to exist in two different types of structures viz. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi></math>-helix and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#946;</mi></math>-pleated sheet structure. These structures arise due to the regular folding of the backbone of the polypeptide&nbsp;chain due to hydrogen bonding between&nbsp;<img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/a1af6b5c-1a61-4333-9087-3b1bc83747c8-9709760.png\" style=\"width: 50px; height: 42px;\" />&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mi>NH</mi><mo>-</mo></math>&nbsp;groups of the peptide bond.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#945;</mi></math>-helix Is one of the most common ways in which a polypeptide chain forms all possible hydrogen bonds by twisting into a right handed screw (helix).</p>",
